    19	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    20	# -------------------------------------
    21	# a) Concrete behavior and trace
    22	#    - The legend stays recognizable from the base task: a 4-neighbor src->tgt pair
    23	#      followed by a teal stencil whose anchor lies one more step in the same
    24	#      direction. That still teaches "source color -> target color" plus one
    25	#      canonical stencil shape.
    26	#    - The changed scattered object is now exactly two cells:
    27	#      {source-colored center} + {fixed marker-color cell in one cardinal neighbor}.
    28	#      The marker no longer acts like another legend color; it is a direction token.
    29	#    - Execution is a causal chain:
    30	#      marker side -> choose stencil orientation -> stamp recolored stencil.
    31	#      Example with a local stencil {(0,0), (1,0), (1,1)} in the legend frame:
    32	#      * base task on source (8,8): stamp stays {(8,8), (9,8), (9,9)}
    33	#      * this combo, marker above source: rotate so "forward" points up, keep it
    34	#        unflipped, then stamp on the source cell
    35	#      * this combo, marker right of source: rotate so "forward" points right, and
    36	#        also mirror because horizontal markers use the mirrored family
    37	#      The visible difference is therefore not a mere shift; the same color can
    38	#      yield different output orientations depending on the nearby marker.
    39	# b) Complexity calibration
    40	#    - Only four marker states exist (up/down/left/right), so the control signal is
    41	#      small and human-visible.
    42	#    - To make the D4 part observable, the generator uses asymmetric stencils and
    43	#      forces each rule to appear with both vertical and horizontal markers. Vertical
    44	#      markers use the unmirrored family; horizontal markers use the mirrored family.
    45	#      This gives repeated evidence for both rotation and reflection without creating
    46	#      eight arbitrary cases.
    47	# c) Hidden assumptions broken in the base solver
    48	#    - Base `_discover_rules` treated every non-bg/non-teal pixel as a possible
    49	#      legend participant. That would misread marker pixels as legend colors, so the
    50	#      fixed marker color must be excluded from legend discovery.
    51	#    - Base rule storage used absolute teal coordinates. That fails once different
    52	#      scattered objects request different orientations, so the learned stencil must
    53	#      be converted into a local frame derived from the legend direction.
    54	#    - Base stamping aligned a raw copied stencil to every source-colored pixel.
    55	#      That is invalid now: only non-legend source pixels with exactly one adjacent
    56	#      marker and no other colored 4-neighbors are true scattered objects.
    57	#    - Base cleanup would preserve the new marker pixels. The combo solver must erase
    58	#      markers together with the source objects and legend region.
    59	# d) Placement legality
    60	#    - The generator places each source+marker pair atomically, checks bounds for
    61	#      both cells, and reserves 4-neighbor padding so no accidental colored adjacency
    62	#      creates false legends or ambiguous source objects.
    63	#    - It also tracks the future output footprint of every transformed stamp, keeping
    64	#      transformed results inside bounds and non-overlapping for visual clarity.
    65	# e) Visual observability
    66	#    - A human sees the same legend-derived stencil reappear around several sources,
    67	#      but the orientation varies only with the marker side.
    68	#    - Because the marker has a fixed dedicated color and repeats across objects, the
